messages:   56%|#####6    | 115/204 [00:40<00:40,  2.19it/s][['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B or C. Please reason step by step.\n####\n# Context:\nIf people work in student jobs on campus, then they need to earn money to help pay for their college tuition. If people order takeout frequently in college, then they work in student jobs on campus. People either order takeout frequently in college or enjoy the dining hall meals and recipes. If people enjoy the dining hall meals and recipes, then they are not picky eaters. If people enjoy the dining hall meals and recipes, then they spend a lot of their time eating and catching up with friends in the campus dining halls. If Mary works in student jobs on campus and needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition, then Mary is neither picky nor needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, is the following statement true, false, or uncertain? Mary either is not a picky eater or, if she is, then she spends a lot of her time eating and catching up with friends in the campus dining halls.\n# Options:\nA) True\nB) False\nC) Uncertain', "To solve this problem, let's break down the given information and analyze it step by step.\n\n1.**If people work in student jobs on campus, then they need to earn money to help pay for their college tuition.**.\n   - This means working in student jobs implies the need to earn money for tuition.\n\n2.**If people order takeout frequently in college, then they work in student jobs on campus.**.\n   - This means frequent takeout implies working in student jobs.\n\n3.**People either order takeout frequently in college or enjoy the dining hall meals and recipes.**.\n   - This means people have to choose one of these two options.\n\n4.**If people enjoy the dining hall meals and recipes, then they are not picky eaters.**.\n   - This means enjoying dining hall meals implies not being a picky eater.\n\n5.**If people enjoy the dining hall meals and recipes, then they spend a lot of their time eating and catching up with friends in the campus dining halls.**.\n   - This means enjoying dining hall meals implies spending a lot of time in the dining halls.\n\n6.**If Mary works in student jobs on campus and needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition, then Mary is neither picky nor needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition.**.\n   - This statement is contradictory because it says Mary needs to earn money but then says she does not need to earn money.This is a paradox and does not provide clear information about Mary's eating habits or her need for money.\nMary is definitely a picky eater and does not spend much time eating or socializing in the campus dining halls.. Therefore, my final option is: "]]
