messages:   59%|#####9    | 121/204 [00:42<00:18,  4.41it/s][['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B or C. Please reason step by step.\n####\n# Context:\nAll employees who schedule a meeting with their customers will appear in the company today. Everyone who has lunch in the company schedules meetings with their customers. Employees will either have lunch in the company or have lunch at home. If an employee has lunch at home, then he/she is working remotely from home. All employees who are in other countries work remotely from home. No managers work remotely from home. James is either a manager and appears in the company today or neither a manager nor appears in the company today.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, is the following statement true, false, or uncertain? James has lunch in the company.\n# Options:\nA) True\nB) False\nC) Uncertain', 'To solve this problem, let\'s break down the information and analyze it step by step:.\n\n1.**All employees who schedule a meeting with their customers will appear in the company today.**.\n   - This means if an employee schedules a meeting, they will be in the company today.\n\n2.**Everyone who has lunch in the company schedules meetings with their customers.**.\n   - This means if an employee has lunch in the company, they must schedule meetings with their customers.\n\n3.**Employees will either have lunch in the company or have lunch at home.**.\n   - This means there are only two options for lunch: in the company or at home.\n\n4.**If an employee has lunch at home, then he/she is working remotely from home.**.\n   - This means having lunch at home implies working remotely.\n\n5.**All employees who are in other countries work remotely from home.**.\n   - This means if an employee is in another country, they work remotely.\n\n6.**No managers work remotely from home.**.\n   - This means managers do not work remotely.\n\n7.**James is either a manager and appears in the company today or neither a manager nor appears in the company today.**.\n   - This means James is in one of two states:.\n     - He is a manager and appears in the company today.\n     - He is not a manager and does not appear in the company today.\nThe statement "James has lunch in the company" is uncertain because James would have lunch at home if he is a manager and not at the company if he is not a manager.. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
